data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"index":0,"function":{"arguments":"{\"kind\":\"build_plan\",\"title\":\"Build Plan: Animated Weather App\",\"content\":\"## In short\\n- **Approach B (rich particle scenes), built on the starter's Bun workspace:** React client, a tiny Hono server that only serves the app, and shared zod schemas. Weather and city data come straight from **Open-Meteo** in the browser. Cities stay **in the browser**.\\n- **8 phases:** shell and theme, data and units, Home figures, search and saved cities, compare, scenes, safeguards and states, install and polish.\\n- **Riskiest part: the full-screen animated canvas on older phones.** Fallback: lower quality steps, then the Simpler effects look, then still shapes. We return to the person before dropping to Approach A.\\n\\n## Approach and stack\\n**Approach B**, as approved in Approaches: realistic canvas scenes with many particles, plus safeguards (quality scaling, pause when hidden, reduce-motion, Simpler effects switch, soft panels). It fits the Constraints: free services, no sign-in, no database of ours, near-zero running cost.\\n\\n**Stack (starter defaults):**\\n- `client/`: Vite, React, React Router, TanStack Query, Tailwind CSS v4, `@corbits/react-ui`, lucide-react.\\n- `server/`: Hono on Bun. It has **no database and no PGlite**: nothing the person enters is stored by us (NFR-10). It serves the built client, the web manifest and a `/health` route. The port comes from the `PORT` environment variable.\\n- `packages/shared`: zod schemas used by the client for Open-Meteo responses, saved cities, settings and the stored weather cache, plus the weather-code mapping.\\n- The design \\\"Adaptive weather-driven\\\" v1 is the visual source: tokens, the `data-wx` palettes, test ids and screen layouts come from `designs/index.html` and `designs/styles.css`.\\n\\n## Components and interfaces\\n\\n**Routes (client):** `/` Home (optionally `?city=` for a preview of an unsaved search result), `/search`, `/cities`, `/compare`, `/settings`. Sidebar nav on all five (drawer under 640px, per design). Use the design's test ids exactly.\\n\\n**Client modules (`client/src/`):**\\n- `app/AppShell.tsx`: `TopBar`, `Sidebar`, `Toaster`, the `data-wx` attribute on the app root, the fixed `WeatherScene` behind content.\\n- `styles/global.css`: design token blocks pasted after the react-ui theme import, then the 17 `[data-wx]` sky and accent sets and day/night surface sets ported from `styles.css`; frosted card blur.\\n- `lib/openMeteo.ts`: `fetchForecast(city)` and `searchCities(q)`; responses parsed with the shared zod schemas.\\n- `lib/weatherMap.ts` (in shared): weather code to one of 7 conditions + intensity (light, moderate, heavy); hot = clear above 30 °C (threshold is one constant); hail and drizzle map to the nearest scene. `sceneKey(city weather)` returns the `data-wx` key using that city's own sunrise and sunset, never the viewer's clock (FR-22).\\n- `lib/units.ts`: one unit setting; °C/km/h/mm vs °F/mph/in; whole degrees with a true minus.\\n- `lib/storage.ts`: localStorage with zod validation: saved cities, unit, simpler effects, last weather per city with time. Corrupt data is dropped, not crashed on.\\n- `lib/useLocation.ts`: browser geolocation; asked on first open and on \\\"Use my location\\\" only.\\n- `hooks/`: `useWeather(city)` (TanStack Query, 5-minute stale time, offline fallback to stored weather), `useCitySearch` (300ms pause), `useSavedCities`, `useSettings`, `useReturnRefresh` (refetch when the tab returns after 15+ minutes).\\n- `scene/WeatherScene.tsx`: one canvas, lazy-loaded (`React.lazy`) so figures never wait on it (FR-32). Files: `engine.ts` (loop, quality level, visibility pause, resize), `quality.ts` (frame-rate monitor), and one effect file per condition (`rain.ts`, `snow.ts`, `wind.ts`, `cloud.ts`, `storm.ts`, `heat.ts`, `fog.ts`), each with day and night colours and an intensity input. Plain-canvas 2D drawing, no engine library, to keep the download small.\\n- `components/`: `StatGrid` usage for conditions, `HourStrip` (24 `li`), `DayList` (7 `li`), `PlacesPanel`, `CityRow`, `CompareCard`, `UnitToggle`, `NoticeCard` (offline, error, denied, preview), `InstallDialog`.\\n- `pages/`: `Home.tsx`, `Search.tsx`, `Cities.tsx`, `Compare.tsx`, `Settings.tsx`.\\n\\n**Server (`server/src/index.ts`):** serves `client/dist`, falls back to `index.html` for client routes, serves `manifest.webmanifest` and `/health`. No API of ours; the browser calls Open-Meteo directly, so traffic is only to the host and the two Open-Meteo services (NFR-10, AC-38).\\n\\n**PWA files (`client/public/`):** `manifest.webmanifest`, icons (192, 512, maskable), and `sw.js` caching the app shell only. Weather data is cached in localStorage, not by the service worker.\\n\\n## Data flow\\n1. **Location:** geolocation gives coordinates; the city name for \\\"My location\\\" comes from the forecast response timezone and a nearest-name lookup through Open-Meteo city search; if none, show \\\"My location\\\".\\n2. **Weather:** one Open-Meteo forecast request per city (current, 24 hourly, 7 daily, sunrise/sunset, weather code, timezone `auto`, units fetched in metric and converted on screen, so toggling units needs no new request). Parsed with zod, cached by TanStack Query for 5 minutes (NFR-13), and the last good result is written to localStorage with its time (IR-9).\\n3. **Scene:** the weather result gives condition, intensity and day/night, which set `data-wx` and the canvas effect. Figures render first; the scene loads after.\\n4. **Search:** typed text, 300ms pause, Open-Meteo geocoding (name, admin1, country, coordinates); order kept as returned. Each result row also loads a small forecast for its temperature and condition.\\n5. **Saved cities and settings:** only in localStorage. Duplicates are identified by rounded coordinates (FR-8).\\n6. **Offline or failure:** if the request fails, show stored weather with its age, and an offline or error notice; if there is none, show the \\\"No saved weather for this city yet\\\" message.\\n7. **Compare selection:** held in the URL (`/compare?c=id,id`) so a reload keeps it; 2 to 4 ids, fifth checkbox disabled.\\n\\n## Phases\\n\\n**Phase 1: Shell, theme, routing.**\\nFiles: `client/src/styles/global.css`, `app/AppShell.tsx`, `pages/*` stubs, `server/src/index.ts`, `client/index.html`.\\nDelivers: layout skeleton, tokens, frosted panels, five routes, sidebar/drawer, responsive layout at 1280/768/360, NFR-8, NFR-2 (focus rings, tab order), IR-1/2/3/4/5 as empty screens, NFR-12 wording style.\\nExit: all five pages open at desktop and phone widths with no sideways scroll; keyboard reaches every nav item.\\n\\n**Phase 2: Data, units, storage.**\\nFiles: `packages/shared/*` schemas and `weatherMap`, `lib/openMeteo.ts`, `lib/units.ts`, `lib/storage.ts`, hooks.\\nDelivers: IR-6, IR-7, IR-9, FR-5, FR-10, FR-11, NFR-13, NFR-11.\\nExit: a console-free dev page prints London's parsed forecast; units convert 0 °C to 32 °F; settings survive reload.\\n\\n**Phase 3: Home figures and states (no scene yet).**\\nFiles: `pages/Home.tsx`, `HourStrip`, `DayList`, `PlacesPanel`, `NoticeCard`, `useLocation`, `useReturnRefresh`.\\nDelivers: FR-1, FR-7, FR-9 (local time), FR-12 to FR-16, FR-33 to FR-38, NFR-3 (row text for rain chance, highs/lows), NFR-6, scene badge text, calm background for loading, denied and empty states.\\nExit: Home shows conditions, 24 hourly rows and 7 daily rows; loading, offline, error, denied, empty and preview states each appear when forced (block network, block location).\\n\\n**Phase 4: Search and saved cities.**\\nFiles: `pages/Search.tsx`, `pages/Cities.tsx`, toast wiring.\\nDelivers: FR-2, FR-3, FR-4, FR-6, FR-7, FR-8, FR-36, IR-2, IR-3, NFR-14 (browser note), Undo toast for 4 seconds.\\nExit: search \\\"Springfield\\\" gives rows with region and country; save, remove with Undo, duplicate blocked; list survives reload.\\n\\n**Phase 5: Compare.**\\nFiles: `pages/Compare.tsx`, `CompareCard`.\\nDelivers: FR-17 to FR-20 (tint ships), IR-4.\\nExit: 2, 3 and 4 cities side by side with +1/+3/+6/+9 hour outlook; fifth blocked; readable at 360px with snap-scrolling inside the grid only.\\n\\n**Phase 6: Animated scenes.**\\nFiles: `scene/*`, `lib/weatherMap.ts` wiring, `data-wx` palette switching.\\nDelivers: FR-21 to FR-27, NFR-1, NFR-4 (lightning capped at 3 flashes a second), full-quality effects per FR-23, intensity levels per FR-25, 1200ms cross-fade (FR-26), `aria-hidden` canvas.\\nExit: a hidden test route `/?force=rain-heavy-night` (any condition, intensity, day/night, also used by checks) shows all 7 conditions by day and night, clearly different; light vs heavy rain differ; city switch fades with no blank flash.\\n\\n**Phase 7: Smoothness safeguards and Settings.**\\nFiles: `scene/quality.ts`, `scene/engine.ts`, `pages/Settings.tsx`.\\nDelivers: FR-28 (3 quality levels plus a still level; step down when frame rate stays under about 30fps for 2 seconds, step up after 10 seconds of recovery), FR-29/NFR-7 (stop the loop on `visibilitychange`), FR-30 (reduce-motion: still sky plus static shapes, no lightning or shimmer, watched live), FR-31 (Simpler effects switch, saved), NFR-5, NFR-15, IR-5, FR-24 at lowest quality, Settings scene gallery of 16 swatches and Open-Meteo credit.\\nExit: throttled CPU drops the particle count within seconds; hidden tab shows no animation work; reduce-motion makes scenes still.\\n\\n**Phase 8: Install and polish.**\\nFiles: `client/public/manifest.webmanifest`, `sw.js`, `InstallDialog`, icons.\\nDelivers: FR-39, NFR-9 pass across browsers (Safari: no install prompt; the dialog shows \\\"Add to Home Screen\\\" steps there), contrast sweep (NFR-1), plain-wording pass (NFR-12), nice-to-haves FR-9, FR-20, NFR-13 all ship.\\nExit: install prompt appears in Chrome/Edge; every checklist item below passes.\\n\\n## Install and run\\n- `bun install` at the repo root.\\n- `bun run dev` starts client and server for development; the port comes from `PORT`.\\n- `bun run build` builds the client; `bun run start` serves it from the Hono server on `PORT`.\\n- No keys, accounts or environment values are needed.\\n\\n## Test plan\\n- **check-app (starter):** builds and starts the app, opens every page at desktop and phone widths, and fails on errors, failed requests, blank pages or sideways scrolling. Run after every phase. Compare's own inner scroll is fine; the page must not scroll sideways.\\n- **Acceptance checks:** try each criterion below in the running app as a person would, using Chrome's device mode and CPU throttling, offline and network blocking, and the forced-scene route for conditions. Also try Firefox, Safari and Edge for AC-37. No test files are added.\\n- Report results by AC id.\\n\\n## Risks and unknowns\\n- **Older phones stutter.** Fallback: the quality steps and Simpler effects; cap particles per level and device pixel ratio at 2 (1 at the lowest level); draw at lower resolution on weak devices. If still not smooth, tell the person before switching to Approach A's lighter scenes.\\n- **Download size.** Fallback: lazy-load the scene, no graphics library, keep code per effect split so only the needed effect loads.\\n- **Text over busy scenes.** Every text sits on a frosted card; if blur is slow on weak devices, switch to a more opaque card at the lowest quality level.\\n- **Open-Meteo limits or changes.** Fallback: 5-minute reuse, and one file (`openMeteo.ts`) to swap for another free provider. Open-Meteo's free service is for non-commercial use; note this credit to the person.\\n- **Name for \\\"My location\\\".** Open-Meteo has no reverse lookup; the plan uses a nearest-name search and falls back to \\\"My location\\\". This is a decision for the person to confirm.\\n- **Safari and iOS:** no install prompt; location needs a user tap on some versions; test on a real phone if possible.\\n- **Browser data cleared** loses saved cities (accepted, NFR-14).\\n- **Scene check by an unlabelled reviewer (AC-18)** needs a human; the forced-scene route supports it.\\n- No requirement is believed wrong or left unreached.\\n\\n## Assumptions to confirm\\n- No server storage or database; the server only hosts the app.\\n- Weather is fetched in the browser directly from Open-Meteo.\\n- Default unit follows the device region (US, Liberia, Myanmar get °F), then is remembered.\\n- Quality thresholds above (30fps, 2 seconds down, 10 seconds up) are the starting values and can be tuned in review.\\n- Compare selection is kept in the web address.\\n\\n## Acceptance checklist\\n- [ ] AC-1: On a fresh browser, open Home and allow location; weather and a matching scene appear, and the prompt does not return on reload.\\n- [ ] AC-2: On Search, type \\\"Springfield\\\"; several results show, each with region and country.\\n- [ ] AC-3: Open a Search result; Home shows its weather, then tap Save city and it joins the saved list.\\n- [ ] AC-4: On Saved cities, remove a city; it vanishes at once and stays gone after reload.\\n- [ ] AC-5: Save two cities, set °F and Simpler effects, close and reopen the browser; all still set.\\n- [ ] AC-6: Saved list shows each city's temperature and condition; tapping a city shows it on Home.\\n- [ ] AC-7: With location allowed, \\\"My location\\\" sits among saved cities; tapping Use my location re-checks it.\\n- [ ] AC-8: Try saving an already saved city from Search or Home; no second entry appears.\\n- [ ] AC-9: Switch to °F; every temperature on every screen changes and 0 °C reads 32 °F.\\n- [ ] AC-10: Switch units; wind flips km/h and mph and rain flips mm and inches.\\n- [ ] AC-11: On Home, all current-condition items show and match Open-Meteo's figures for the city.\\n- [ ] AC-12: The \\\"Next 24 hours\\\" strip has 24 entries from the current hour, each with time, temperature, condition and chance of rain.\\n- [ ] AC-13: \\\"Next 7 days\\\" lists 7 days with day, condition, high, low and chance of rain.\\n- [ ] AC-14: Last-updated time shows; leave the tab 15+ minutes (or simulate it), return, and the weather refreshes unprompted.\\n- [ ] AC-15: Tap Refresh; data reloads and the updated time changes.\\n- [ ] AC-16: On Saved cities tick 2, 3 and 4 cities and open Compare; all show side by side with data, icons and outlook; the fifth checkbox is disabled.\\n- [ ] AC-17: At 360px wide, Compare is readable with no cut-off text.\\n- [ ] AC-18: Force each of the seven conditions; each scene looks distinct, and an unlabelled reviewer can tell them apart, also with Simpler effects on.\\n- [ ] AC-19: View Sydney and London at the same moment; their day and night scenes follow each city, not the viewer's clock.\\n- [ ] AC-20: At full quality on desktop: rain shows drops and splashes, snow shows depth, storm shows lightning, hot clear shows shimmer, wind moves clouds and objects, fog shows mist.\\n- [ ] AC-21: Force light and heavy rain; their density is clearly different.\\n- [ ] AC-22: Switch between a sunny and a rainy city; the scene fades with no blank flash.\\n- [ ] AC-23: Measure text contrast over every scene, day and night; at least 4.5:1.\\n- [ ] AC-24: Throttle the CPU; the particle count drops within a few seconds and the frame rate recovers; on a mid-range phone it holds about 30fps.\\n- [ ] AC-25: Switch tabs; the performance tool shows animation work stop, and it resumes on return.\\n- [ ] AC-26: With reduce-motion on, no scene falls, flashes or shimmers yet the weather is identifiable; storm at full quality never flashes more than 3 times a second.\\n- [ ] AC-27: Turn on Simpler effects; particles visibly drop and the setting persists after reload.\\n- [ ] AC-28: On a throttled mobile connection, figures show within 3 seconds, before the scene finishes loading.\\n- [ ] AC-29: On a slow connection, Home shows placeholders, never a blank screen.\\n- [ ] AC-30: View a city, go offline, reopen; its last weather shows with its age; an unseen city shows the offline message.\\n- [ ] AC-31: Block location; a plain message appears, search is offered, and saved cities still show.\\n- [ ] AC-32: Search \\\"qzxwv\\\"; a no-results message with suggestions appears.\\n- [ ] AC-33: Block the weather service; a plain error and Try again appear, and retry works once unblocked.\\n- [ ] AC-34: First visit, location declined, nothing saved; Home shows the search prompt on a calm background.\\n- [ ] AC-35: The browser offers install from Settings; the installed app opens full screen.\\n- [ ] AC-36: Using only the keyboard, search, save, compare, toggle units and change settings; a screen reader announces conditions, forecast, comparison and the scene text.\\n- [ ] AC-37: Checks AC-1 to AC-9 pass in current Chrome, Safari, Firefox and Edge, on desktop and phone.\\n- [ ] AC-38: Network traffic shows requests only to the host and Open-Meteo's weather and city services; no sign-in is requested.\\n- [ ] AC-39: A non-technical reviewer reads every message and finds none confusing.\\n- [ ] AC-40: Settings shows the browser-only saving note and every listed setting.\\n- [ ] AC-41: On a deliberately weak test device, numbers, forecasts and Compare all work with a simpler scene.\\n- [ ] AC-42: Home, Search and Saved cities exist with their listed contents.\\n\"}"}}]},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{},"finish_reason":"tool_calls"}]}

